ed, vengeful, with the prisoners turned crew. The standoff in the cave, by torchlight, with a treasure chart between them. 'Share?' the mutineer Sykes suggested. Captain Will Hawkins laughed. Pirates share. Up to a point.\n\nInland: jungle, then ruins, then the cave. a treasure chart's X marked a chamber sealed a century. Inside: not gold — at first. A tomb. A logbook. The real a treasure chart: the captain's confession, naming every cache along Port Royal."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "It ended as these things do: the mutineer Sykes overreached, Captain Will Hawkins didn't, and the sea took the mutineer Sykes with the tide. Captain Will Hawkins stood in the surf, breathing hard, a treasure chart safe. 'Bury them decent,' Captain Will Hawkins ordered. Pirates have rules.\n\nThey sailed for Port Royal with the gold and a treasure chart and the logbook's secrets. The crew's shares would make them rich. Captain Will Hawkins's share would make them legend. the gunner was already spending theirs on a tavern.\n\nOff the headland, Captain Will Hawkins opened the logbook one last time. So many caches. So many voyages. the sea tests all — the sea had tested it, and it held."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Port and Pardon', 'text': "Captain Will Hawkins took the logbook to the governor — not the mutineer Sykes's governor, the new one — and traded certain pages for a pardon, signed and sealed. The crew cheered. Respectability, it turns out, is its own adventure.\n\nPort Royal welcomed them with bells and rum. The gold was divided fair — the articles are the articles. a treasure chart's copies went to every crew member who'd earned one. The original stayed with Captain Will Hawkins.\n\na treasure chart hangs in the tavern now, framed, with the articles beneath it. Tourists gawk. Old sailors nod. the sea tests all — carved, at the gunner's insistence, on the frame."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Tide Returns', 'text': "the mutineer Sykes's successor was out there somewhere — there's always a successor. Captain Will Hawkins wasn't worried. The sea decides, and the sea loves the bold.\n\nPort Royal shrank astern again, beloved and boring in the best way. Ahead: a treasure chart, the sea, the test. Captain Will Hawkins stood at the wheel and felt the old lift. This is the life.\n\nA year later, a new a treasure chart surfaced — they always do — and Captain Will Hawkins was packed before the ink dried. the gunner closed the tavern for 'renovations' and came along, because that's what friends do."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

